id: rule-whilet-never-stops
title: A loop that never stops
topic: Loop
example-of: WHILE-TOTAL
derived: false

task:
  Use the rule WHILE-TOTAL to try to show that the loop terminates, with
  the invariant $x >= 0$. Choose the candidate variant above the steps.
  The logical variable $n$ is the value of $E$ when an iteration starts.

given:
  Loop condition B: $x >= 0$
  n: the value of $E$ when an iteration starts

pre: x >= 0
post: x >= 0 && !(x >= 0)

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while x >= 0 do {
    {I && x >= 0}
    {R1}
    x := x + 1     // b
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(x >= 0)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

choice: E = x
outcome: fails

step: The invariant I and the variant E
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: while
text:
  The rule uses the precondition as the invariant. We try the variant
  $E = x$.
set: I = x >= 0

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: x >= 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  $P$ and $I$ are the same formula.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the body for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN
focus: b
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant. We substitute the variable $x$
  with $x + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, I)
  = (x >= 0)[x <- x + 1]     // I is x >= 0
  = x + 1 >= 0               // x replaced by x + 1
set: R1 = x + 1 >= 0

step: I and B imply R1
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && x >= 0, R1
text:
  >>> «I» && «x >= 0 : B» ==> «R1»
vc: x >= 0 && x >= 0 ==> x + 1 >= 0
why:
  From $x >= 0$ we get $x + 1 >= 0$. The invariant is preserved.

step: The body must decrease E
part: Variant
rule: WHILE-TOTAL, ASSIGN
focus: b
text:
  The body must make $E$ smaller than its value $n$ at the start of the
  iteration. We compute the weakest precondition of $x < n$ for the body.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, x < n)
  = (x < n)[x <- x + 1]
  = x + 1 < n          // x replaced by x + 1
vc: x >= 0 && x >= 0 && x = n ==> x + 1 < n
status: fails
counterexample: x = 0, n = 0
why:
  With $x = n$, $x + 1 = n + 1$ is not smaller than $n$: the body
  increases $E$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && !(x >= 0), Q
text:
  >>> «I» && «!(x >= 0) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: x >= 0 && !(x >= 0) ==> x >= 0 && !(x >= 0)
why:
  The two formulas are the same: $Q$ is $I && !B$. Partial correctness
  holds, but the variant failed, so termination is not proved.

choice: E = -x
outcome: fails

step: The invariant I and the variant E
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: while
text:
  The rule uses the precondition as the invariant. We try the variant
  $E = -x$, which decreases in every iteration.
set: I = x >= 0

step: P implies I
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, I
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «I»
vc: x >= 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  $P$ and $I$ are the same formula.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the body for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN
focus: b
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant. We substitute the variable $x$
  with $x + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, I)
  = (x >= 0)[x <- x + 1]     // I is x >= 0
  = x + 1 >= 0               // x replaced by x + 1
set: R1 = x + 1 >= 0

step: I and B imply R1
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && x >= 0, R1
text:
  >>> «I» && «x >= 0 : B» ==> «R1»
vc: x >= 0 && x >= 0 ==> x + 1 >= 0
why:
  From $x >= 0$ we get $x + 1 >= 0$. The invariant is preserved.

step: The variant must not be negative
part: Variant
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
focus: I && x >= 0
text:
  When an iteration starts, $E$ must not be negative.
  >>> «I» && «x >= 0 : B» ==> «-x >= 0 : E ≥ 0»
vc: x >= 0 && x >= 0 ==> -x >= 0
status: fails
counterexample: x = 1
why:
  In the state $x = 1$, $-x = -1$. No variant can work, because the loop
  never stops: $x$ only grows, so $x >= 0$ stays true.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: I && !(x >= 0), Q
text:
  >>> «I» && «!(x >= 0) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: x >= 0 && !(x >= 0) ==> x >= 0 && !(x >= 0)
why:
  The two formulas are the same: $Q$ is $I && !B$. Partial correctness
  holds, but the variant failed, so termination is not proved.
